---
/*
  /lab/page-card — a LOCAL-ONLY bench, dev-gated below, linked from nowhere.

  THE QUESTION, Michael 2026-08-10: *"what it looks like if my writing fragments
  are put into a card of sorts, so that it's like reading like it conveys it's a
  page of something. That way it's styled a little differently than the quotes
  within the constellation views."*

  So this bench is not "cards vs typeset". `.suite` settled that in 2026-07-23
  (admin.css: "Typeset, not carded … each medium in its own register") and the
  settlement is what makes the question askable: **a page IS a register.** A
  quote is set whole and finishes on the screen; an essay here is a title and a
  fading excerpt whose text is somewhere else. Giving the incomplete one an edge
  says out loud what the lamplight and the "Read →" already say by implication —
  this is a leaf of a longer thing. Quotes stay bare, and get MORE distinct by
  the contrast, which is the actual ask.

  ⚠ NOTHING SHIPPED IS TOUCHED. Every variant is CSS over ConstellationSuite's
  real DOM, hung off a wrapper class — no prop, no fork, no branch in
  SuiteStanza. That is deliberate twice over: the bench renders the REAL room
  (real arcs, real lamplight, real Reader, real 68ch measure), and the whole
  experiment deletes in one file if the answer is no.

  ⚠ AND IT RENDERS IN SITU, NOT SIDE BY SIDE. A gallery of three cards on a
  blank page would answer a question nobody asked. The thing to judge is a card
  ARRIVING between two bare quotes under a passing light, so the suite is real
  and the variants swap in place — a difference this small is only visible when
  nothing else moves.

  THE VARIANTS (the sheet changes; the chrome is held constant on purpose)
    leaf   — hairline edge, paper tint, footer rule. The card at its most
             restrained; the one that could ship as-is.
    recto  — no edge at all: tint + a lift. Tests the house rule head-on
             (`--depth: 0`, "flat. restraint IS the warmth"), because a sheet
             LYING ON something is the strongest page signal there is and the
             rule forbids exactly that. Look at it in both themes — a shadow
             is nearly mute on dusk's near-black, so the lift here is a
             shadow below AND a hairline highlight above.
    stack  — leaf, plus two sheet edges peeking behind. The literal reading of
             "a page OF something": page one of several.

  WHAT TO ACTUALLY LOOK FOR — three things this will probably break:
    1. THE FIGURE. The arcs bow up to 30px off the marks and the stanzas paint
       ABOVE the line (`.suite-item` z-1, `.suite-line` z-0), so an opaque sheet
       EATS the constellation where it passes. Paper is translucent here by
       default for that reason; `opaque paper` is the toggle that shows you the
       damage. `stack` ignores it and is always opaque — a stack of translucent
       sheets is mud, which is a finding, not an oversight.
    2. THE RAGGED LEFT. `--indent` offsets every stanza (0–15%) because typeset
       lines can wander. Cards are objects, and objects read as misaligned
       rather than as drifting. `flush left` zeroes it for writing only — the
       arcs just re-measure, so this is a real option and not a cheat.
    3. THE MEASURE. The card is capped near the 68ch text measure rather than
       filling the column: a page-shaped thing wants to be portrait-ish, and a
       56rem-wide sheet holding 36rem of text is a banner.

  ⚠ THE EXCERPT GREW TO 5 LINES, and only because the container asked for it: 4
  lines is a teaser, and a page with four lines on it is not a page. It stops at
  5 rather than going further because the excerpt is capped at 400 chars
  (lib/blog.ts) — at 6+ lines most essays stop truncating and the fade would be
  a mask over text that was never cut, which is a lie about there being more.

  ⚠ THE RADIUS IS 3px, NOT `--radius-box`. 0.75rem is what a UI card has; a cut
  sheet of paper has a nearly hard corner. This is the single clearest line
  between "my writing is in a card" and "my writing is on a page", so it is
  worth the deviation from the token — and worth stating that it IS one.
*/
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import ConstellationSuite from '../../components/ConstellationSuite.astro';
import { getConstellation, listConstellations } from '../../lib/constellations';

if (import.meta.env.PROD) return Astro.redirect('/', 302);

const supabase = Astro.locals.supabase;

/* `the-proving-ground` by default because it is the hardest case in the corpus:
   8 essays interleaved with 7 quotes, so every card lands next to the bare
   register it is meant to differ from. `?c=<slug>` drives any other suite —
   `conditions-not-character` (7 essays, no quotes) is the useful control for
   "does a wall of cards still read as a suite?" */
const slug = Astro.url.searchParams.get('c') || 'the-proving-ground';
const [constellation, all] = await Promise.all([getConstellation(supabase, slug), listConstellations(supabase)]);
if (!constellation || constellation.items.length === 0) return Astro.redirect('/', 302);

const VARIANTS = [
  ['off', 'off — as it ships'],
  ['leaf', 'leaf — hairline edge'],
  ['recto', 'recto — no edge, a lift'],
  ['stack', 'stack — page one of several'],
] as const;
---

<SiteLayout title="Page card — bench" active="home" backToTop={false}>
  <div class="pc pc-off" data-pc-room>
    <ConstellationSuite constellation={constellation} />
  </div>

  {
    /* The controls sit OUTSIDE the room, fixed, bottom-left — the suite's own
      floating ✦ holds bottom-right. Deliberately utilitarian: bench chrome
      should never be mistakeable for something the site has. */
  }
  <form class="pc-panel" data-pc-panel aria-label="Bench controls">
    <fieldset class="pc-panel__set">
      <legend class="pc-panel__legend">the sheet</legend>
      {
        VARIANTS.map(([value, label], i) => (
          <label class="pc-panel__row">
            <input type="radio" name="variant" value={value} checked={i === 0} />
            {label}
          </label>
        ))
      }
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset class="pc-panel__set">
      <legend class="pc-panel__legend">and then</legend>
      <label class="pc-panel__row">
        <input type="checkbox" name="opaque" />
        opaque paper <span class="pc-panel__hint">(watch the arcs)</span>
      </label>
      <label class="pc-panel__row">
        <input type="checkbox" name="flush" />
        flush left <span class="pc-panel__hint">(drop --indent)</span>
      </label>
    </fieldset>

    {
      /* ⚠ `__set` ALONE, never `__set __row`. Wearing both put a column flex
        (`__set`) under `align-items: center` (`__row`), and a <select> sizes to
        its longest option — "to be like the rock that the waves keep crashing
        over" — so it grew past the 15rem panel and hung off both sides of it,
        the left one straight off the screen. `min-width: 0` could not save it:
        that shrinks flex ITEMS along the main axis, and in a column the main
        axis is height. */
    }
    <label class="pc-panel__set">
      <span class="pc-panel__legend">suite</span>
      <select name="c" data-pc-suite>
        {
          all.map((c) => (
            <option value={c.slug} selected={c.slug === constellation.slug}>
              {c.name}
            </option>
          ))
        }
      </select>
    </label>
  </form>
</SiteLayout>

{
  /* is:global because every selector reaches into ConstellationSuite's DOM,
  which Astro's scoping cannot see. Safe by construction: this file is the only
  page that loads this stylesheet, and every rule is under `.pc-*`. */
}
<style is:global>
  .pc {
    /* ── bench-local tokens ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
       ⚠ THE SHADOW COLOUR IS A RAW COLOUR, and app.css's first law forbids
       exactly that ("Never write a raw color at a usage site"). It is declared
       HERE, once, named, so the violation is visible and so the fix is obvious
       if `recto` ever wins: a shadow token in app.css, not eight call sites.
       There is no legal alternative today — the only dark slot on hand is
       base-content, which on dusk is cream, and a cream shadow is a glow. */
    --pc-shade: oklch(0% 0 0 / 0.2);
    --pc-width: 38rem;
    --pc-radius: 3px;
    --pc-pad: 1.6rem;
    --pc-edge: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 13%, transparent);
    /* Translucent by default so the drawn figure survives underneath. */
    --pc-paper: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-200) 62%, transparent);
  }
  .pc.pc-opaque {
    --pc-paper: var(--color-base-200);
  }
  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .pc {
      --pc-pad: 2rem;
    }
  }

  /* The sheet. `> .grow` is ConstellationSuite's text column — the ONE wrapper
     that already holds the title, the excerpt AND the "Read →", so the card can
     enclose the whole stanza without a single change to the component. The
     margin gutter (`.suite-mark`) stays outside it, which is what keeps the star
     beside the page rather than on it. */
  .pc-leaf .suite-writing > .grow,
  .pc-recto .suite-writing > .grow,
  .pc-stack .suite-writing > .grow {
    position: relative;
    max-width: var(--pc-width);
    padding: var(--pc-pad) var(--pc-pad) calc(var(--pc-pad) * 0.7);
    background: var(--pc-paper);
    border-radius: var(--pc-radius);
  }

  .pc-leaf .suite-writing > .grow,
  .pc-stack .suite-writing > .grow {
    border: 1px solid var(--pc-edge);
  }

  /* recto — no edge. A sheet lying on the surface, which needs BOTH halves of a
     lift to survive two themes: the shadow does the work on paper, the top
     highlight does it on dusk where a black shadow is invisible. */
  .pc-recto .suite-writing > .grow {
    border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 9%, transparent);
    box-shadow:
      0 1px 2px var(--pc-shade),
      0 14px 34px -14px var(--pc-shade);
  }

  /* stack — two sheet edges behind the first. z-index -1 lands them behind
     `.grow`'s own background but inside `.suite-item`'s stacking context (z-1),
     so they cannot fall behind the arcs; the sheets are opaque for the same
     reason the front one is here — layered translucency is mud. */
  .pc-stack {
    --pc-paper: var(--color-base-200);
  }
  .pc-stack .suite-writing > .grow::before,
  .pc-stack .suite-writing > .grow::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    z-index: -1;
    inset: 0;
    background: var(--color-base-200);
    border: 1px solid var(--pc-edge);
    border-radius: var(--pc-radius);
  }
  .pc-stack .suite-writing > .grow::before {
    transform: translate(4px, 4px);
  }
  .pc-stack .suite-writing > .grow::after {
    transform: translate(8px, 8px);
  }

  /* A page carries more than a teaser — see the header for why it stops at 5. */
  .pc-leaf .suite-writing .sw-excerpt,
  .pc-recto .suite-writing .sw-excerpt,
  .pc-stack .suite-writing .sw-excerpt {
    -webkit-line-clamp: 5;
  }

  /* The turn, at the outer corner: a footer rule and the word pushed right, so
     "Read →" reads as turning the page rather than as a link under a paragraph.
     Held IDENTICAL across all three variants — only the sheet is under test. */
  .pc-leaf .suite-writing > .grow > .suite-cta,
  .pc-recto .suite-writing > .grow > .suite-cta,
  .pc-stack .suite-writing > .grow > .suite-cta {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    margin-top: 1.15rem;
    padding-top: 0.75rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--pc-edge);
  }

  /* Cards are objects, and objects read as misaligned rather than as drifting.
     Beats `.suite-item`'s own rule on specificity alone — no !important. */
  .pc-flush .suite-writing {
    margin-inline-start: 0;
  }

  /* ── the panel ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .pc-panel {
    position: fixed;
    bottom: 1rem;
    left: 1rem;
    z-index: 40;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    max-width: 15rem;
    padding: 0.85rem 0.95rem;
    background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-200) 92%, transparent);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--radius-box);
    backdrop-filter: blur(6px);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 75%, transparent);
  }
  .pc-panel__set {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.2rem;
    border: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .pc-panel__legend {
    padding: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    font-variant: all-small-caps;
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 45%, transparent);
  }
  .pc-panel__row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.45rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .pc-panel__hint {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 38%, transparent);
  }
  .pc-panel select {
    /* `width` + `border-box`, not `flex: 1` — the option list is what sizes a
       <select>, so the width has to be imposed rather than negotiated. */
    width: 100%;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    background: var(--color-base-100);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--radius-field);
    padding: 0.15rem 0.3rem;
    color: inherit;
    font: inherit;
  }
</style>

<script>
  /* The whole bench is class-swapping on one wrapper — that is the point. The
     suite behind it never re-renders, so a variant change moves ONLY the sheet
     and the eye can trust what it just saw change. */
  const room = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-pc-room]');
  const panel = document.querySelector<HTMLFormElement>('[data-pc-panel]');

  function apply() {
    if (!room || !panel) return;
    const data = new FormData(panel);
    const variant = String(data.get('variant') ?? 'off');
    room.className = ['pc', `pc-${variant}`, data.get('opaque') && 'pc-opaque', data.get('flush') && 'pc-flush']
      .filter(Boolean)
      .join(' ');
    /* NO REDRAW IS ASKED FOR HERE, and it took reading the script to know that.
       Padding and a 5th line change every stanza's height, so the drawn figure
       is instantly measuring a layout that no longer exists — but
       `constellation-suite.ts` observes `document.body` with a ResizeObserver
       and calls `drawLine()` from it, and taller stanzas make a taller body. A
       synthetic `resize` here would only re-run the glow (the window listener
       is wired to `onScroll`, not to the redraw), so it would look like the fix
       while being none of it. */
  }

  panel?.addEventListener('change', (e) => {
    /* The suite picker is navigation, not a class — it needs a different room. */
    if ((e.target as HTMLElement)?.dataset.pcSuite !== undefined) {
      const to = new FormData(panel).get('c');
      window.location.search = `?c=${to}`;
      return;
    }
    apply();
  });
</script>
